Subject: [PATCH 2/3] MemoryCore: apply retention decay when re-ranking L1 search hits

The search path merges FTS5 and vector results with plain RRF. That means a
memory recalled yesterday and a memory untouched for two months land in the same
order given equal textual relevance. Adding a bounded multiplier changes the order
and nothing else: no candidate is dropped, because a forking path that loses
recall is worse than one that ranks imperfectly.

Design choices worth flagging in review:

  - The multiplier is a factor in [0.3, 1.5], applied to the fused score. The
    floor comes from mem0's documented decay feature; the ceiling stops one hot
    memory from burying everything else.
  - A hard pin (priority = -1, or an explicit metadata pin) is treated as
    permanently fresh: multiplier 1.0. Not 1.5, measured: promoting pins to the
    ceiling cost 5 points of precision@10 against an embedding-derived ground
    truth, because mediocre pinned memories displaced genuinely better matches.
  - The age term stands down entirely when the corpus has no access history
    (cold start). Age is only evidence of disuse if someone is recording use.
    On a corpus with no access data, forcing decay cost 3.5 points of precision.

